                <pb facs="0620" n="525" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg525"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13734: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-11-19T11:00:00+08:00">November 19, 1936—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 19—6:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>558. Embassy’s 540, November 12, 4 p.m. Tientsin communicated notice to Americans
                    resident in Suiyuan and Ningsia and advised them if they found themselves
                    endangered, or if the situation gave promise of developing in such a way as to
                    threaten their position, they should withdraw to a place of safety. Several
                    Americans have already withdrawn from Suiyuan. No report from Hankow.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
